so this is the second film you've  directed the first was Coriolanus a few  years ago when you signed on to work on  this film was it always going to be in  the form of directing it and acting in  it or were you at first just going to  direct it well I think the first thing  to say is I I was quite I didn't expect  after making Coriolanus I'd certainly  hoped I might direct something again but  I didn't know what it was going to be I  never imagined that I would be doing a  victorian-era drama centered around  Charles Dickens and Nellie Ternan but I  was given this screenplay by Abby Morgan  and and then I liked it very much in it  I had I knew very little about Charles  Dickens and you'll be surprised to hear  I hadn't studied Charles Dickens at  school  lots of Shakespeare but no Dickens and I  I read the screenplay and I was very  intrigued by the story by her - by  Nellie and then I was so intrigued I  immediately got hold of the book which  is a brilliant book and if anything in  this film is interested you at all I  really urge you to buy this book because  it's it's really interesting about  actresses in the 19th century theater  and it starts off setting the scene for  what it was like to be an actress in the  19th century and then introduces the  turn and the history of the turn and  family the mother played by Kristen and  it's a fantastic piece of research and  very very accessible not not at all dry  or or difficult to to become involved in  and so that that really got under my  skin and this lots of reasons really one  of the things that moved me was Nellie  the story of Nellie and particularly in  Abbey screenplay the idea that there's a  woman looking for closure about a past  love a past intimate relationship and  although of course it's about Charles  Dickens and that's another element that  was fascinating to me I didn't know much  about Dickens and Claire Tomalin  description of Dickens this crazy highly  energized famous writer but there's so  many things about him that are  interesting and of course in this this  part of Dickens his life that we see we  see it and a side of Dickens that  perhaps well I didn't know much of  which was the very abrupt and possibly  cruel way he left his family and his  wife for Nellie and that's always when  when biographers write about Dickens and  they get to this bit they usually and  they're usually admiring and pretty in  love in love with Dickens but when they  get to this part of his life they they  find it hard to grasp the nettle but one  of the things that I love when I watch  films is what the sound not thus the the  soundscape is doing and how that can add  a lot of atmosphere and tension but I  just was experimenting really with ways  of creating atmosphere or somehow  ambushing the audience's expectations I  actually preferred less music in films  to more and but rather this the sound  I would rather emphasize the sound  escape of a film than the music and so  some of these places might be places  normally that you'd put music in but I  actually just I think I just said let's  see what happens if we just take the  sound out because it gives you a sense  of where are we what's happening it  doesn't yeah it's where it suppose it's  a storytelling atmospheric thing that  just I just came across and liked it  even though there have been multiple  films made based on Dickens as work if  I'm not mistaken there hasn't been a  film made about his life do you have a  feeling or an opinion on why that is  no I don't it's really odd do you'd  think that because his life is amazing  his total life is certainly rich in in  in indeed in things that would you think  would make a movie when I couldn't find  any movie where Charles Dickens the man  was Wood had been had been recreated and  interpreted so I don't know why that is  I don't know why it's it's it's  surprising we spoke a couple of days ago  in front of an audience with felicity as  well and I asked her a question about  the two sides to her performance the the  younger side the older side the Nelly  versus the  Allen I'd be curious to know how you as  a director helped her craft the two  sides of her performance because she  really did see them as distinct sides  one of the things that I admired admire  and admired about Felicity was her  ability to sort of really inhabit an  interior world within herself and I  wanted an actress who I felt could by  simply what she was doing in in here and  here could suggest a young a young girl  and then this older older Nelly who's in  her late thirties early forties and she  has this sense of sort of reserved about  her which I really liked  because of scheduling reasons and  availability of actors and all kinds of  things we had to shoot all the Margate  scenes the beat that the school scenes  first so it was not ideal because I  clearly she would love to have played  younger Nellie and then the older Nellie  so but funnily enough it was that she  seemed to have uncanny access to that  the older Nellie I mean there were like  the scene in the the graveyard at the  end which was you know difficult scene  she hadn't been able to play the whole  part before she so but she she was  stunning in that scene the way I believe  that that's an extraordinary piece of  screen acting when she has to speak  literally on the page it's what you  would call a speech and she has to  inhabit it and find it and I ever and  she did three or four takes which I  thought all had amazing things in them I  think it was more tricky playing the  younger Nellie we had to so chart the  peaks and the troughs the highs and the  lows of when she would crack when there  would be moments we would see her her  her vulnerability and have fragility and  that was something that we both sort of  had to figure out a bit more but she  always came extraordinarily prepared and  ready and always sometimes I think on  set the adrenaline makes me probably a  bit  sometimes bit brusque but she was always  very tolerant and always very courageous  when I would be pushing for her to  explore something else and I think  that's the thing that I like as an actor  is that I am pushed to go to areas I  hadn't anticipated and she was certainly  incredibly courageous in trying to honor  what I was asking of her is that a  change in the dynamic where the actor is  calming the director down now they were  raising their eyes to haven't know  what's he on about now no no I mean I  was I was blessed with an amazing crew  and cast who you know I believe and  they're not they're not here to  contradict me but I I believe there was  a very good collective spirit a  collective sense of common endeavor on  the film and clearly there were days I  mean I think there were definitely days  when you know you're wrestling with that  Kristin Scott Thomas who's extremely got  a very highly refined sense of what is  truth what's truthful for her and I have  a you know she's an old friend and I  have huge respect for her she would  quite rightly challenge me on on  something line that didn't feel quite  right that that a phrase or a New  Orleans or something she would say I'm  not sure this is right and I'd say well  okay let's find let's find a phrase or  something that would work and I mean you  know you you there are healthfully there  are good creative moments which are  sometimes full of a healthy kind of  tension which is the debate of trying to  get to the heart of something and those  and you know I'm like I learned I'm  learning that those things you know they  come at you and you're directing and  actors say I don't understand why am i  doing why do I have to do that and  that's and that's what I would probably  ask certainly I would probably
 

